1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,310
To retire with a million pounds,

2
00:00:02,330 --> 00:00:05,940
starting at 25, you need about £329

3
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:07,070
a month going in.

4
00:00:07,370 --> 00:00:09,270
And here's the bit nobody tells you:

5
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:12,700
about £123 of that doesn't come out of

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:13,520
your pocket.

7
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:16,940
And I know a million sounds like a lottery number.

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:17,890
It did to me.

9
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:20,460
But when you actually break it down,

10
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:23,290
it's a monthly direct debit and a lot of patience.

11
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:25,380
And most of it isn't even your money.

12
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:28,540
So I want to show you who pays the rest,

13
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:30,000
what happens to that number if you're

14
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:33,200
starting at 35 or 45, and the one thing

15
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:35,200
that quietly takes a quarter of a million

16
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:36,020
off the end.

17
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:38,240
And then I'll show you where I actually am

18
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:39,060
with mine.

19
00:00:41,730 --> 00:00:43,450
Right, the number first.

20
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:46,030
I've assumed your money grows about 7% a

21
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:47,870
year, which is roughly what a global

22
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:49,360
tracker has done over the long run.

23
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:51,740
It's not a promise, some years are horrible.

24
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:56,150
Start at 25 and it's £329 a month.

25
00:00:56,550 --> 00:00:59,260
Start at 35 and it's £700.

26
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:02,290
Start at 45 and it's about sixteen hundred

27
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:03,340
pounds a month.

28
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:05,670
So every ten years you wait,

29
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:07,730
the monthly bill more than doubles.

30
00:01:07,990 --> 00:01:09,500
That's the whole game, honestly.

31
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:10,930
It's not about being clever,

32
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:12,390
it's about being early.

33
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:14,240
And look at this.

34
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:17,180
From 25, over the whole 42 years,

35
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:20,630
what actually goes in is about 166 grand.

36
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:23,970
The other 834 grand is just growth.

37
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:26,420
The money made more money than you did.

38
00:01:27,370 --> 00:01:29,600
And this is the bit that makes people quit.

39
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:32,530
The first hundred grand takes nearly 15 years.

40
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:34,130
It feels like nothing's happening.

41
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:36,480
But the last hundred grand,

42
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,060
from 900 to a million, takes about 17 months.

43
00:01:40,130 --> 00:01:41,550
Same money going in.

44
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:43,450
So if you're a few years in and it feels

45
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:44,950
slow, that's normal.

46
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:46,490
That's what it's meant to feel like.

47
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:49,440
And what does a million actually pay you?

48
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:51,950
The rule of thumb is you can take about 4%

49
00:01:51,970 --> 00:01:52,510
a year.

50
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:54,270
So that's around 40 grand a year,

51
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:56,140
and the pot should roughly last.

52
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:57,750
I did a whole video on whether that's

53
00:01:57,770 --> 00:01:59,230
enough, and I'll come back to the catch in

54
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:00,180
a minute.

55
00:02:01,550 --> 00:02:04,390
But 329 quid a month is still a lot when

56
00:02:04,410 --> 00:02:05,240
you're 25.

57
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:07,340
So here's where it gets cheaper.

58
00:02:08,410 --> 00:02:10,340
Say you're on 35 grand.

59
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:12,210
Your workplace pension already puts in

60
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:14,360
around £192 a month.

61
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:17,240
But only about £96 of that is yours.

62
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:19,790
Your employer puts in about £72,

63
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:22,100
and the taxman adds about £24.

64
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:24,170
Then you top up the gap yourself,

65
00:02:24,190 --> 00:02:26,220
and the taxman adds to that as well.

66
00:02:26,930 --> 00:02:31,430
So for £329 going in, about £206

67
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:32,390
comes out of your pay.

68
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:35,720
The other £123 is free money.

69
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:38,650
So the cheapest way, by a mile,

70
00:02:38,670 --> 00:02:40,340
starts with the pension at work.

71
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:42,250
If your employer matches more when you put

72
00:02:42,270 --> 00:02:44,490
in more, take every pound of it before you

73
00:02:44,510 --> 00:02:45,640
do anything else.

74
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:47,980
Nothing else you can buy gives you that.

75
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:51,440
And if 329 feels like too much right now,

76
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:52,720
there's another way to do it.

77
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:55,560
Start lower, and put it up a bit every year.

78
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:58,230
If you raise it by 3% a year,

79
00:02:58,250 --> 00:03:00,260
basically every time you get a pay rise,

80
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:03,490
you can start at about £223 a month and

81
00:03:03,510 --> 00:03:04,980
still get to the same place.

82
00:03:05,670 --> 00:03:07,190
That's the trick I actually like.

83
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:09,880
You never see the money, so you never miss it.

84
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:12,110
Most workplace pensions let you change the

85
00:03:12,130 --> 00:03:13,800
percentage in about two minutes.

86
00:03:14,530 --> 00:03:16,990
After that, it's a stocks and shares ISA.

87
00:03:17,230 --> 00:03:18,910
You don't get anything on the way in,

88
00:03:18,930 --> 00:03:21,120
but there's no tax at all on the way out,

89
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:23,300
and you can get at it whenever you need to.

90
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:26,140
You can put in up to 20 grand a year,

91
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:28,640
which is more than most of us will ever manage.

92
00:03:29,390 --> 00:03:30,810
And what goes inside it?

93
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:32,200
For me it's boring.

94
00:03:32,270 --> 00:03:34,750
One cheap tracker fund that buys the whole market.

95
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:36,800
I'm not picking winners, I tried that,

96
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:38,640
and I'll tell you how that went.

97
00:03:40,030 --> 00:03:41,720
But what if you're not 25.

98
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:44,350
What if you're 35, or 45,

99
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:46,230
and you're looking at that chart thinking,

100
00:03:46,250 --> 00:03:47,660
well, that's me done.

101
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:49,750
It's not done.

102
00:03:49,990 --> 00:03:52,510
At 45, a million needs about sixteen

103
00:03:52,530 --> 00:03:54,650
hundred a month, which is a lot.

104
00:03:54,670 --> 00:03:57,250
But half a million needs about £800.

105
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:58,850
And if your employer's paying in,

106
00:03:58,870 --> 00:04:00,750
a good chunk of that isn't yours either.

107
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:03,440
And you're not starting from nothing.

108
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:07,210
The full State Pension is £241.30 a week

109
00:04:07,230 --> 00:04:07,930
right now.

110
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:09,530
That's about twelve and a half grand a

111
00:04:09,550 --> 00:04:11,250
year on top of whatever you've built,

112
00:04:11,270 --> 00:04:12,650
as long as you've got enough years of

113
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:13,710
National Insurance.

114
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:16,970
So half a million, plus the State Pension,

115
00:04:16,990 --> 00:04:19,050
is still a really comfortable retirement.

116
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:21,400
Starting late doesn't mean you've failed.

117
00:04:21,450 --> 00:04:23,540
It just means the number's different.

118
00:04:24,910 --> 00:04:26,640
Now the bit that ruins it.

119
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:27,880
And it's not a crash.

120
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:29,640
It's fees.

121
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:32,560
Same money in, same 42 years.

122
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:34,770
But if you're paying 1% a year more in

123
00:04:34,790 --> 00:04:36,890
fees, you don't end up with a million.

124
00:04:37,010 --> 00:04:39,740
You end up with about 746 grand.

125
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:41,870
That's a quarter of a million pounds,

126
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:44,540
gone, for a number most people never look at.

127
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,790
So if you do one thing after this video,

128
00:04:47,910 --> 00:04:50,260
log in to your pension and find the fee.

129
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:52,510
A lot of old workplace pensions are sat in

130
00:04:52,530 --> 00:04:55,070
expensive funds and nobody's ever checked.

131
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,650
The other thing that ruins it is you.

132
00:04:58,670 --> 00:04:59,580
Well, me.

133
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,000
The market will drop, probably by a lot,

134
00:05:02,110 --> 00:05:04,260
probably more than once in 40 years.

135
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,930
The 7% only works if you're still in when

136
00:05:06,950 --> 00:05:07,800
it comes back.

137
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:10,720
What helps me is not looking.

138
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:12,800
I check mine about once a month now.

139
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:14,550
When I was checking it every day,

140
00:05:14,590 --> 00:05:16,800
every red day felt like an emergency,

141
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:18,680
and that's when you do something stupid.

142
00:05:19,710 --> 00:05:21,010
So that's the whole thing,

143
00:05:21,030 --> 00:05:22,870
really. Take the employer money first.

144
00:05:22,890 --> 00:05:25,440
Then an ISA, in one cheap tracker.

145
00:05:25,530 --> 00:05:26,450
Check the fee.

146
00:05:26,630 --> 00:05:28,140
Put it up when your pay goes up.

147
00:05:28,170 --> 00:05:29,810
And don't sell when it drops.

148
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:31,050
None of it's clever.

149
00:05:31,070 --> 00:05:33,400
It's just hard to keep doing for 40 years.

150
00:05:34,450 --> 00:05:36,510
And I've got to be honest about one more thing.

151
00:05:36,790 --> 00:05:39,680
A million in 42 years isn't a million today.

152
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:42,330
With prices going up about 2% a year,

153
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:45,840
it buys what roughly 435 grand buys now.

154
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:48,630
That's still a really good retirement.

155
00:05:48,710 --> 00:05:49,920
It's just not a yacht.

156
00:05:51,710 --> 00:05:53,930
Right, so where am I with this.

157
00:05:53,970 --> 00:05:55,540
Because I said I'd show you.

158
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:58,450
I started investing at 22.

159
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:00,000
And I went bust.

160
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,480
I lost all of it.

161
00:06:01,510 --> 00:06:03,430
So if that chart made you feel behind,

162
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:05,660
I basically started again from zero.

163
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:07,870
I'm 27 now.

164
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:11,270
I put £500 a month into an S&P 500 fund,

165
00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:13,550
and I've got about four grand in there so far.

166
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:16,230
Most of what I save isn't even for

167
00:06:16,250 --> 00:06:17,060
retirement yet.

168
00:06:17,270 --> 00:06:19,310
A grand a month goes into savings for a

169
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:20,670
house, because that's the thing in front

170
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:21,650
of me right now.

171
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:24,760
So just on that £500.

172
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:26,650
If I keep it up, and the market does

173
00:06:26,670 --> 00:06:28,030
roughly what it's done before,

174
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:31,740
that gets me to about 1.38 million by 67.

175
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:34,870
It'd cross the million at about 63.

176
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:36,880
So yeah, I'm on track.

177
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:39,620
But only if I don't do what I did at 22,

178
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:40,970
and that's the hard part,

179
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:41,920
not the maths.

180
00:06:42,930 --> 00:06:45,740
I'm not a financial advisor and this isn't advice.

181
00:06:45,830 --> 00:06:47,410
Pensions have rules about when you can

182
00:06:47,430 --> 00:06:48,970
take the money and how it's taxed,

183
00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:50,780
so check what applies to you.

184
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:52,990
Tell me in the comments what age you

185
00:06:53,010 --> 00:06:54,910
started, or what age you're starting.

186
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:56,510
And if you want to know whether a million

187
00:06:56,530 --> 00:06:58,490
is even enough, I did that one already,

188
00:06:58,510 --> 00:06:59,490
it's on screen now.
